        "id": "thm-ip-sparse-detection-regime-split",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "title": "Sparse q<2 detection regime split",
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice and the minimax detection-risk definition with $0<q<2$, $a_j=j^\\alpha$, $\\sigma_j=j^\\beta$, and $\\alpha,\\beta>0$. Let $r_\\varepsilon\\to0$ as $\\varepsilon\\downarrow0$, and put\n$$\\lambda=\\frac{\\alpha+\\beta}{2}-\\frac{\\beta}{q}.$$\nFor $\\lambda>0$, put $A=2\\alpha+1/q-1/2$, $B=2(\\alpha+\\beta)+1/q$, and $D=\\alpha+\\beta(1-2/q)>0$. Choose any $\\delta_\\varepsilon>0$ with $\\delta_\\varepsilon\\to0$ and $\\delta_\\varepsilon\\log\\varepsilon^{-1}\\to\\infty$, set $\\widetilde r_\\varepsilon=r_\\varepsilon(1-\\delta_\\varepsilon)$, and define\n$$u_\\varepsilon^2=2\\inf\\left\\{\\sum_{j\\ge1}h_j^2\\sinh^2(z_j^2/2):\\ 0\\le h_j\\le1,\\ z_j\\ge0,\\ \\sum_{j\\ge1}j^{2\\beta}h_jz_j^2\\ge\\frac{\\widetilde r_\\varepsilon^2}{\\varepsilon^2},\\ \\sum_{j\\ge1}j^{q(\\alpha+\\beta)}h_jz_j^q\\le\\frac1{\\varepsilon^q}\\right\\}.$$\nFor each fixed significance level $\\ell\\in(0,1)$, the sharp errors have Gaussian form\n$$\\beta_\\varepsilon(\\Theta_q(r_\\varepsilon),\\ell)=\\Phi(H_\\ell-u_\\varepsilon)+o(1),\\qquad \\gamma_\\varepsilon(\\Theta_q(r_\\varepsilon))=2\\Phi(-u_\\varepsilon/2)+o(1),$$\nwhere $\\Phi(t)=(2\\pi)^{-1/2}\\int_{-\\infty}^t e^{-x^2/2}\\,dx$ and $H_\\ell$ is its $(1-\\ell)$-quantile. Moreover, if $r_\\varepsilon=o(\\varepsilon^{a_c})$ with $a_c=(q\\alpha+1-q/2)/(q(\\alpha+\\beta)+1)$, then\n$$u_\\varepsilon\\sim c_{\\alpha,\\beta,q}\\,\\varepsilon^{-A/D}r_\\varepsilon^{B/D}$$\nfor a constant $c_{\\alpha,\\beta,q}>0$. Without this additional restriction on $r_\\varepsilon$, the sharp error formulas above remain valid for every $r_\\varepsilon\\to0$, and a minimax separation rate is\n$$r_\\varepsilon^*=\\varepsilon^{A/B}.$$\nIf $\\lambda\\le0$, for the same fixed significance level $\\ell\\in(0,1)$ put $n_\\varepsilon=r_\\varepsilon^{-1/\\alpha}$ and $D_\\varepsilon=n_\\varepsilon^{-\\beta}r_\\varepsilon/\\varepsilon-\\sqrt{2\\log n_\\varepsilon}$. Then the sharp errors have degenerate form\n$$\\beta_\\varepsilon(\\Theta_q(r_\\varepsilon),\\ell)=(1-\\ell)\\Phi(-D_\\varepsilon)+o(1),\\qquad \\gamma_\\varepsilon(\\Theta_q(r_\\varepsilon))=\\Phi(-D_\\varepsilon)+o(1),$$\nand a sharp separation-rate normalization is\n$$r_\\varepsilon^*=\\Lambda\\,\\varepsilon^{\\alpha/(\\alpha+\\beta)}(\\log\\varepsilon^{-1})^{\\alpha/[2(\\alpha+\\beta)]},\\qquad \\Lambda=\\left(\\frac{2}{\\alpha+\\beta}\\right)^{\\alpha/[2(\\alpha+\\beta)]}.$$\nThus the sparse boundary $\\lambda=0$ belongs to the degenerate regime, and the two regimes have different separation rates.\n\nThe deterministic globalized score-and-maximum family and the finite maximum tests constructed in the proof are asymptotically minimax in the Gaussian and degenerate regimes, respectively; the significance tests include deterministic randomization to satisfy the exact level constraint.",
